# g95-fu-residual-b: what the wiring applies (core)

Library side is done in mixdog-core-domains `domains/desktop_residual_b.rs` (no hot file touched). Four call sites need the lead's files.

## 1. Cargo (hot files): two new dependency edges of mixdog-core-domains

`interface_addresses()` (the interfaces of the machine, so that an adapter joining without a route change fires the network watcher) and the
login-shell read's `setsid` call platform APIs. Both crates are already in Cargo.lock and the workspace.

native/core/crates/mixdog-core-domains/Cargo.toml, after the `[dependencies]` table:

    [target.'cfg(unix)'.dependencies]
    libc = { workspace = true }

    [target.'cfg(windows)'.dependencies]
    windows-sys = { workspace = true, features = ["Win32_Foundation", "Win32_NetworkManagement_IpHelper", "Win32_NetworkManagement_Ndis", "Win32_Networking_WinSock"] }

Consequences the lead regenerates (not written by hand): the `mixdog-core-domains` entry of native/core/Cargo.lock gains `libc` and
`windows-sys 0.61.x`; mixdog-workspace-hack gains the feature `Win32_NetworkManagement_Ndis` on its windows-sys 0.61 line (`GetAdaptersAddresses`
and `IP_ADAPTER_ADDRESSES_LH` need it with `Win32_Networking_WinSock`; the other three features are already there).

## 2. Network watcher for the relay leg (finding #11, #95)

native/core/crates/mixdog-app-server-daemon/src/compose_domains/rows.rs, in `build`, directly after the line
`let power = Arc::new(RemotePower::new(seams.remote.clone(), seams.local_target.clone()));`:

    // network-watch.ts: the leg redials at once when the machine comes back online or its addresses change (the old main process reported
    // `power('resume')` for it; `RemotePower` closes the leg and redials from the first delay, as a system resume does).
    let on_network_change = power.clone();
    drop(mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::NetworkWatcher::start(
        Duration::from_millis(mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::NETWORK_POLL_MS),
        Arc::new(mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::network_snapshot),
        Arc::new(move || on_network_change.power(mixdog_app_protocol::v2::host::PowerEvent::Resume)),
    ));

(`mixdog_core_domains::domains::host::PowerSink` must be in scope in rows.rs for `.power(..)`; `PowerEvent` is the one adapters.rs imports; `Duration` is std::time.) The watcher
is a mixdog-rt task that lives for the process, as the TypeScript timer did. After it, desktop `index.ts` `createNetworkWatcher({...})` and
network-watch.ts (+ test) are deleted by the client task (x-residual-moves-b-client).

## 3. Import progress (finding #10)

The call site named in the plan (node_calls.rs) cannot hold it: mixdog-app-server cannot depend on mixdog-core-domains (it is the other way), and
node_calls.rs only classifies method names into lanes. The call site is a decorator over the `import/*` service, so that every caller of the
three methods is seen: `ImportProgressService::new(inner, announce)` forwards every call and passes the result of `import/startConversationImport`,
`import/cancelConversationImport` and `import/getConversationImportJobs` to `ImportProgress::observe`; the poll asks the same service for the job
list every `CONVERSATION_IMPORT_POLL_MS` while a job runs.

native/core/crates/mixdog-app-server-daemon/src/compose_domains.rs, `compose_domains_post`, replace

    services.push(Arc::new(mixdog_services::import::ImportService::new(discovery, jobs, mixdog_services::import::Limits::default())));

with

    let import = Arc::new(mixdog_services::import::ImportService::new(discovery, jobs, mixdog_services::import::Limits::default()));
    let announce_publisher = state.publisher.clone();   // whatever carries an app-feed notification, see below
    services.push(Arc::new(mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::ImportProgressService::new(
        import,
        Arc::new(move |_event, job| { /* announce `import/progress/updated` { job } on the app feed, the way desktopPet/state/changed is announced */ }),
    )));

The announce closure needs a protocol notification, which no table has yet (the desktop event `conversation-import-progress` was an Electron IPC
event; the renderer listens to it through contract-ipc.ts). Rows for the protocol wiring (the lead pastes them; new type in a new v2 module,
e.g. `v2/import_progress.rs`):

    [notifications]
    # after UiSettingsChanged in notification_definitions! (app feed, delivered to every window and paired device like settings-changed):
    ImportProgressUpdated => "import/progress/updated" (ImportProgressUpdatedParams) @app,
    # v2/import_progress.rs:  pub struct ImportProgressUpdatedParams { pub job: JsonValue }   (the job snapshot exactly as
    # import/getConversationImportJobs returns it: jobId, seq, state, processed, ...), derives as UiSettingsChangedParams.
    [free_text]
    # the job's string fields are free text: add `import/progress/updated /job/**` to the payloads of free_text_fields.json.

The desktop client task (x-residual-moves-b-client) maps this notification to the renderer's `conversation-import-progress` handler and then
deletes conversation-import-progress.ts (+ its test in conversation-import.test.mjs); until then the TypeScript twin and this decorator would both
publish, so the compose line and the TypeScript deletion land in the same wave. The decorator needs no entry in SERVICE_PENDING (it serves the
methods `ImportService` serves, the same names).

## 4. Login-shell environment (finding #12)

Call site chosen: the start of `mixdog_app_server_daemon::run()` in native/core/crates/mixdog-app-server-daemon/src/lib.rs, before
`boot_executors()`: no executor thread exists yet, the daemon already depends on mixdog-core-domains (no new edge), and the core, every terminal
and every tool call inherit the process environment. The shell is read only where the old main process read it: macOS and Linux (Windows returns
an empty adoption). First statements of `run()`:

    // login-shell-environment.ts: a Dock, Finder or desktop-entry launch lacks what the user's profile exports.
    let inherited: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, String> = std::env::vars().collect();
    let adoption = mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::adopt_login_shell_environment(
        mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual::host_platform(),
        &inherited,
        mixdog_core_domains::domains::desktop_residual_b::LOGIN_SHELL_READ_BUDGET,
    );
    for (name, value) in &adoption.set {
        // SAFETY: nothing else runs yet: the executors and every thread that reads the environment are created below.
        unsafe { std::env::set_var(name, value) };
    }
    // the boot diagnostic `login-shell-environment`: duration, adoption.adopted, adoption.changed_count, adoption.homebrew_path_added,
    // adoption.failures (the fields the old writeBootDiagnostic wrote); a failed read is also logged once at error level here.

`adoption.set` holds the shell's overrides (the probe's PWD/OLDPWD/SHLVL/_, Electron's switches and `MIXDOG_*` values the launcher already
set are left alone) with Homebrew's directories appended to `PATH` on macOS. Decision left to the lead: whether a daemon started from a terminal by
the CLI (not by the desktop) also reads the shell (the old code read it for every desktop start only); gate it on the fact the desktop start
carries today, if there is one, rather than on an environment switch. Then desktop index.ts `adoptLoginShellEnvironment` / `loginShellEnvironmentReady`
and login-shell-environment.ts (+ test) are deleted by the client task.

## 5. Attachments (findings #213, #214)

One reader already: `DesktopAttachmentHost` (mixdog-core-session `session/attachments.rs`) answers the residual services' `AttachmentHost` and the
blob domain's `SessionAttachments` over `desktop_residual::named_attachment` / `session_attachments`, and its three unit tests exist
(rows named, history beyond the window, bytes by hash). compose_flip.rs builds three instances of the same stateless adapter
(`PostSeams.session_attachments`, `DesktopResidualService::new`, `DesktopResidualBService::new`); optional tidy-up for the lead: build one
`Arc<DesktopAttachmentHost>` and clone it into the three.
